※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=14 diag=none

※ The arms stand where the statements of a body would, in either of the two
※ notations the language has for a block.  An arm is a pattern and then a body
※ written the way a function's is, so that what a body looks like is one thing
※ wherever one appears; inside braces the arms follow one another with nothing
※ between them, each ending in the brace that closes it.

let hundred: mut u8 = 100u8
let seven: mut u8 = 7u8

@[visible]
let kept: mut u8 = 0u8

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u8 { match hundred ÷ seven { u8(q) { kept ← q } ⊥ { kept ← 9u8 } } ; kept }
